sealed secrets

The infrastructure is ready. Here is how you Seal that configuration so you can safely push it to GitHub:

The "Sealing" Workflow
Once you have installed the controller and the kubeseal CLI (from the previous step), run these commands on your master node:

bash
# 1. Create a temporary "Standard" Secret (DO NOT PUSH THIS TO GITHUB)
kubectl create secret generic alertmanager-prometheus-kube-prometheus-alertmanager \
  --from-file=alertmanager.yaml=alertmanager-config.yaml \
  -n monitoring --dry-run=client -o yaml > temp-alertmanager-secret.yaml
# 2. Encrypt (Seal) the secret using the controller's public key
# This creates a file that is SAFE to push to GitHub
kubeseal --format=yaml < temp-alertmanager-secret.yaml > alertmanager-sealed.yaml
# 3. DELETE the temporary unencrypted secret
rm temp-alertmanager-secret.yaml
Now, you can push it:
bash
git add alertmanager-sealed.yaml
git commit -m "feat: add sealed alertmanager config"
git push origin test
What about your Main App Secrets?
You can do the exact same thing for your auth-service or project-service secrets (like DB passwords):

Create the secret locally: kubectl create secret generic my-app-secret --from-literal=password=123... --dry-run=client -o yaml > secret.yaml
Seal it: kubeseal < secret.yaml > sealed-secret.yaml
Push the SealedSecret to Git.
ArgoCD will see the SealedSecret, and the controller in the cluster will automatically turn it back into a normal Secret for your pods to use.

